is positive, negative or neutral. It is a prediction with uncertainty, because sarcasm and context can mislead it.", "cv_what": "Computer vision lets computers analyse images. An image is stored as numbers, a model finds patterns in them, and it outputs labels, detected objects or descriptions.", "cv_pixels": "A pixel is the smallest dot of an image. In colour images each pixel has three numbers, the red, green and blue channels, usually from 0 to 255.", "cv_patch": "Vision transformers cut an image into small square patches, flatten each patch into numbers (a 4x4 RGB patch gives 48), and multiply by a learned matrix to get a vector. Attention then compares the patches.", "cv_detect": "Image classification gives the whole image one label. Object detection finds each object and draws a bounding box around it. A confidence score is the model's own certainty, and it can still be wrong.", "tr_split": "Data is split into a training set (fit the model), a validation set (tune settings) and a test set (one final honest score on unseen data). Keeping them separate reveals overfitting.", "tr_over": "Overfitting is when a model memorises its training data and does badly on new data. Underfitting is when it is too simple to learn the pattern. Fixes include more data, simpler models, limiting tree depth and validating on held-out data.", "tr_metrics": "Accuracy is the share of correct predictions. Precision is how many predicted positives were right, recall is how many real positives were found, and F1 combines the two. A confusion matrix shows all the counts.", "tr_kinds": "Prompting only gives instructions to a finished model; that is inference, not training. Fine-tuning updates a model's weights with new examples. Training from scratch builds a model from random weights.", "sys_confidence": "I keep a small set of learned knowledge about AI basics. For each question I compute a similarity-based confidence score; when it is above a threshold calibrated on held-out examples I a